/-
# Automorphisms of prime order with centralizer of finite rank (Kourovka Notebook Problem 13.58)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 13.58 (E. I. Khukhro): let `φ` be an
automorphism of prime order `p` of a nilpotent periodic group `G` with `C_G(φ)` of finite sectional
rank `r`. Does `G` have a normal subgroup `N`, nilpotent of class bounded by a function of `p`, with
`G / N` of finite sectional rank bounded in terms of `r` and `p`?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Nilpotent

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PrimeOrderAutomorphismRankNilpotentKourovka13Problem58Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 13.58 (E. I.
Khukhro). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700976 (`KOU-13.58`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Rank at most `r` is "every finitely generated subgroup is generated by at most `r` elements". In a
periodic nilpotent group every finitely generated subgroup is a finite nilpotent group, whose
minimal number of generators is the maximum over its Sylow subgroups, so this is the sectional rank;
`C_G(φ)` and `G / N` are again periodic nilpotent. Nilpotent of class at most `c` is
`upperCentralSeries N c = ⊤`; `orderOf φ = p` in `G ≃* G`.
-/

/-- `G` has rank at most `r`: every finitely generated subgroup of `G` is generated by at most `r`
elements. For the periodic nilpotent groups used below this is the sectional rank. -/
def RankLE (G : Type*) [Group G] (r : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ H : Subgroup G, H.FG → ∃ S : Finset G, S.card ≤ r ∧ Subgroup.closure (S : Set G) = H

/-- The centralizer `C_G(φ)` of an automorphism `φ`: its subgroup of fixed points. -/
def fixedSubgroup {G : Type*} [Group G] (φ : G ≃* G) : Subgroup G :=
  (φ : G →* G).eqLocus (MonoidHom.id G)

/-- Kourovka 13.58 (E. I. Khukhro): there are functions `f(p)` and `g(r, p)` such that if `φ` is an
automorphism of prime order `p` of a periodic nilpotent group `G` and `C_G(φ)` has finite sectional
rank `r`, then `G` has a normal subgroup `N`, nilpotent of class at most `f(p)`, such that `G / N`
has sectional rank at most `g(r, p)`. -/
def nilpotentByBoundedRankOfCentralizerRank : Prop :=
  ∃ (f : ℕ → ℕ) (g : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ), ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], Group.IsNilpotent G →
    (∀ x : G, IsOfFinOrder x) → ∀ (φ : G ≃* G) (p r : ℕ), p.Prime → orderOf φ = p →
      RankLE (fixedSubgroup φ) r →
      ∃ (N : Subgroup G) (_ : N.Normal), Subgroup.upperCentralSeries N (f p) = ⊤ ∧
        RankLE (G ⧸ N) (g r p)

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 13.58 "Yes, it does", citing Khukhro (the case `p = 2`
was proved earlier by Shumyatsky): `G` has a normal subgroup `N` of `p`-bounded nilpotency class
with `G / N` of `(r, p)`-bounded sectional rank. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.),
Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026),
Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 13.58 (E. I. Khukhro); resolution: E. I. Khukhro, J. London
Math. Soc. 77 (2008), 130-148 (the case p = 2: P. Shumyatsky, Arch. Math. 71 (1998), 425-432),
Kourovka Archive, Problem 13.58 (answer: Yes, it does).
-/
public theorem_wanted nilpotentByBoundedRankOfCentralizerRank_holds :
    nilpotentByBoundedRankOfCentralizerRank

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.PrimeOrderAutomorphismRankNilpotentKourovka13Problem58Wanted
